1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,140
There's a real cost difference between putting something in your global instructions, which you pay for on every single message, and putting it in a skill, which you only pay for when it's actually relevant.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,000
This episode is about skills.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,352
Automation, Skills, New skill.

4
00:00:08,352 --> 00:00:12,500
Three fields: a name, a one-line description, and a Markdown body with the actual procedure.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:18,000
The description is the part the model always sees; the body only loads in when the description matches what's being asked.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,167
The worked example: a sql-conventions skill.

7
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:25,091
Its body holds your team's actual rules — table naming, migration format, how to write a safe query.

8
00:00:25,091 --> 00:00:31,000
It sits there unused in every unrelated chat, and loads in fully the moment someone asks for a query or a schema change.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The one rule that makes or breaks a skill: the description must say when to use it, not what's inside it.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,190
Contains our SQL style guide tells the model nothing about which moment to reach for it.

11
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:45,000
Use this when writing or reviewing any SQL query or migration is specific enough to trigger reliably.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,007
Three ways to hold information, and they're not interchangeable.

13
00:00:48,007 --> 00:00:50,544
A skill is a procedure, loaded in whole when relevant.

14
00:00:50,544 --> 00:00:53,832
Knowledge is large material, searched piece by piece at question time.

15
00:00:53,832 --> 00:00:59,000
Memory is small facts about you or the project, injected into every single message whether it's needed or not.

16
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
And memory, the third kind: small facts, always injected, on every single message — the most expensive place to put anything long.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,185
You've now met agents, swarms, and skills individually.

18
00:01:10,185 --> 00:01:14,000
Next we chain steps together into a workflow, so one step's output feeds directly into the next.

